{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f55aac6dba0f4d8bbecfafdabd5a5523","ordinal":361,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.120","text":"\n\nBut it is impossible to conceive of a thing that cannot be known before the thing it must be apprehended before. So it is impossible to conceive of something that is both relative and revelatory of the very thing in relation to which it is thought. But they say that the sign is both relative and revelatory of the thing signified. So it is impossible to conceive of the sign."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-491971662b8f43f6b89035c040614db7","ordinal":362,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.121","text":"\n\nThis too must be said. There has been disagreement among our predecessors: some say that there is an indicative sign, others assert that there is no indicative sign. So whoever says that there is an indicative sign will say so either simply and without proof, using bare assertion, or with proof. If he uses mere assertion, he will not be believed. If he wants to prove it, he will be taking for granted what is in question."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-824e3fb2981b4322a650d597d6baecdc","ordinal":363,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.122","text":"\n\nFor proof is said to be, generically, a sign. So if it is disputed whether there is a sign or not, it will also be disputed whether there is proof at all. Suppose, for example, that we were investigating whether there is such a thing as an animal. We would also be investigating whether there is such a thing as a human being, since a human being is an animal. But it is absurd to prove what is in question through what is equally in question, or through itself. So no one will be able to affirm by proof either that there is a sign."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-291c5003f0f34485aab534c118c5c856","ordinal":364,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.123","text":"\n\nAnd if it is possible to make a firm declaration about the sign neither simply nor with proof, it is impossible to make an apprehensive statement about it. And if the sign is not apprehended with precision, it will not be said to signify anything either, since there is no agreement even about the sign itself. For this reason it will not even be a sign. So by this reasoning too the sign will be unreal and inconceivable."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3d9b84a8003b452c820c234ce64726f9","ordinal":365,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.124","text":"\n\nFurther, this too must be said. Signs are either only apparent or only non-evident, or some signs are apparent and some non-evident. None of these is sound, so there is no sign. That not all signs are non-evident is shown as follows. The non-evident does not appear of itself, as the dogmatists say, but is perceived through something else. So the sign too, if it were non-evident, will need another non-evident sign, since on the hypothesis before us nothing apparent is a sign; and that sign will need another, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to take in infinitely many signs. So it is impossible for the sign to be apprehended if it is non-evident. For this reason it will also be unreal: since it is not apprehended, it cannot signify anything or be a sign."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-39addd111a3a4d9db694dbab4e9e5d4a","ordinal":366,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.125","text":"\n\nSuppose all signs are apparent. The sign is relative, and relative to the thing signified, and relatives are apprehended together with one another. So the things said to be signified, being apprehended together with apparent things, will be apparent. When right and left strike us at the same time, the right is said to appear no more than the left, and the left no more than the right. In the same way, when the sign and the thing signified are apprehended together, we must say that the sign appears no more than the thing signified."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8e8003f568734ed6bf2733117b133c53","ordinal":367,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.126","text":"\n\nAnd if the thing signified is apparent, it will not even be signified, since it does not need anything to signify and reveal it. When right is abolished, there is no left either; in the same way, when the thing signified is abolished, there can be no sign either. So the sign turns out to be unreal if one says that signs are only apparent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-90b3cbdc27c241bd8cd105f6457dc0f1","ordinal":368,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.127","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that some signs are apparent and some non-evident. Even so the difficulties remain. The things said to be signified by the apparent signs will be apparent, as we said before. Since they do not need anything to signify them, they will not be signified at all. Hence those signs will not be signs either, since they signify nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a4e28f6c5e5349a29bca3e9d7a7555ad","ordinal":369,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.128","text":"\n\nAs for the non-evident signs, they need things to reveal them. If they are said to be signified by non-evident things, the argument falls into infinite regress and they turn out to be inapprehensible, and for that reason unreal, as we said before. If they are said to be signified by apparent things, they will be apparent, since they are apprehended together with their apparent signs; and for that reason they will be unreal too. For it is impossible for anything to be both non-evident by nature and apparent. Yet the signs under discussion, though assumed to be non-evident, have been found to be apparent as the argument turns itself around."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8c9832ca54f743a6a7d8a0abe4efae26","ordinal":370,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.129","text":"\n\nIf, then, signs are neither all apparent nor all non-evident, nor some apparent and some non-evident, and there is nothing besides these, as they themselves say, the so-called signs will be unreal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3ac14c35f30c43aca9f101cb74b3ef82","ordinal":371,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.130","text":"\n\nThese few points out of many will suffice for now to suggest that there is no indicative sign. Next we will also set out the considerations suggesting that there is a sign, so as to show the equipollence of the opposing arguments. The utterances brought against the sign either signify something or signify nothing. If they signify nothing, how could they shake the existence of the sign?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fe23bce1753b4607bcb79ecd0a2a42be","ordinal":372,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.131","text":"\n\nIf they signify something, there is a sign. Again, the arguments against the sign are either probative or not probative. If they are not probative, they do not prove that there is no sign. If they are probative, then, since proof is generically a sign, revealing its conclusion, there will be a sign. Hence an argument of this kind is propounded: if there is a sign, there is a sign; and if there is not a sign, there is a sign, since that there is not a sign is shown by proof, which is a sign. Either there is a sign or there is not a sign; therefore there is a sign."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-21aa97d3c2a14baf8f4290e3b016d34e","ordinal":373,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.132","text":"\n\nAlongside this argument is set one of this kind. If there is not any sign, there is not a sign; and if there is a sign, of the kind the dogmatists say there is, there is not a sign. For the sign under discussion is said, by its very concept, to be both relative and revelatory of the thing signified, and it turns out to be unreal, as we showed."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b87fab5531dc42ca8592924f7f2c3aa6","ordinal":374,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.133","text":"\n\nEither there is a sign or there is not a sign; therefore there is not a sign. And as for the utterances on behalf of the sign, let the dogmatists themselves answer whether they signify something or signify nothing. If they signify nothing, they do not confirm that there is a sign. If they signify something, the thing signified will follow from them. And that was that there is a sign; from which it follows that there is ⟨not⟩ one, as we pointed out, as the argument turns itself around. However, since such plausible arguments are brought both for there being a sign and for there not being one, we must say that there is “no more” a sign than not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-28a307ee82a54eb08ae283a2719938a8","ordinal":375,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.134","text":"\n\nIt is plain from this that proof is not something agreed upon either. If we suspend judgment about the sign, and proof is a kind of sign, we must suspend judgment about proof as well. Indeed, we will find that the arguments propounded about the sign can be applied against proof too. Proof too is thought to be relative and revelatory of its conclusion, and almost everything we said against the sign followed from these features."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1849acb5d6444a9a9195e94e7c8e81dc","ordinal":376,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.135","text":"\n\nBut if we must also speak about proof specifically, I will go through the argument about it concisely, first trying to make clear briefly what they say proof is. Proof, then, as they say, is an argument that, by means of agreed premises, reveals a non-evident conclusion by way of deduction. What they mean will become clearer from what follows. An argument is a system made up of premises and a conclusion."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9583f2d1dcf440ca993252313818864c","ordinal":377,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.136","text":"\n\nIts premises are said to be the propositions adopted by agreement for establishing the conclusion, and the conclusion [conclusion] is the proposition established from the premises. For instance, in this ⟨argument⟩, “If it is day, it is light; but it is day; therefore it is light,” “therefore it is light” is the conclusion, and the rest are premises."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c9807571d2d6437db17b917b15628bba","ordinal":378,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.137","text":"\n\nOf arguments, some are conclusive and some inconclusive. They are conclusive when the conditional that begins with the conjunction of the argument’s premises and ends in its conclusion is sound. The argument just mentioned, for example, is conclusive, since “it is light” follows from the conjunction of its premises, “it is day” and “if it is day, it is light,” in this conditional: “[if] it is day, and if it is day, it is light.” Inconclusive arguments are those that are not like this."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e897e03779df4af899c226fedb0cec93","ordinal":379,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.138","text":"\n\nOf conclusive arguments, some are true and some not true. They are true when not only the conditional formed from the conjunction of the premises and the conclusion is sound, as we said before, but also the conclusion is true, and so is the conjunction of its premises, which is the antecedent in the conditional. A true conjunction is one all of whose parts are true, like “it is day, and if it is day, it is light.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b1945eb903d049928032e3d0c9dcb8b3","ordinal":380,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.139","text":"\n\nNot true are those that are not like this. Take an argument like “If it is night, it is dark; but it is night; therefore it is dark.” It is conclusive, since this conditional is sound: “[if] it is night, and if it is night, it is dark, ⟨it is dark; therefore⟩ it is dark.” It is not, however, true. The conjunction “it is night, and if it is night, it is dark” is false, because it contains the false “it is night”; for a conjunction that contains something false is false. Hence they also say that a true argument is one that deduces a true conclusion through true premises."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0f8ca81be05d4dbd855f59bdcaf41484","ordinal":381,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.140","text":"\n\nAgain, of true arguments, some are probative and some not probative. Probative are those that deduce something non-evident through evident things; not probative are those that do not. For example, an argument like “If it is day, it is light; but it is day; therefore it is light” is not probative, since its conclusion, that it is light, is evident. But an argument like “If sweat flows through the surface, there are intelligible pores; but sweat flows through the surface; therefore there are intelligible pores” is probative, since its conclusion, “therefore there are intelligible pores,” is non-evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-21ef0217d6f44ad6a6b8e913a9261344","ordinal":382,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.141","text":"\n\nOf those that deduce something non-evident, some lead us through the premises to the conclusion merely by way of progression; others do so by way of progression and of revelation at once. Arguments by way of progression are those that seem to depend on trust and memory, such as this one: “If someone has told you that this man will be rich, this man will be rich; this god here (I point, for the sake of argument, to Zeus) has told you that this man will be rich; therefore this man will be rich.” We assent to the conclusion not so much because of the necessity of the premises as because we trust the god’s declaration."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0641e3ee310941ca9e86ee2e6397bad9","ordinal":383,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.142","text":"\n\nOthers lead us to the conclusion not only by way of progression but also by way of revelation, like this one: “If sweat flows through the surface, there are intelligible pores. But the first; therefore the second.” Sweat’s flowing reveals that there are pores, because it has been assumed beforehand that liquid cannot pass through a solid body."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ad9407602db14e26aea1a3d6f1837738","ordinal":384,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.143","text":"\n\nProof, then, must be an argument; it must be conclusive and true; and it must have a non-evident conclusion [and] one revealed by the power of the premises. That is why proof is said to be an argument that, by means of agreed premises, reveals a non-evident conclusion by way of deduction. This, then, is how they usually explain the concept of proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2bb6757db2eb432bb85fe4cd065381cf","ordinal":385,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.144","text":"\n\nThat proof is unreal can be inferred from what they themselves say, by overturning each of the elements included in its concept. For instance, an argument is composed of propositions. But composite things cannot exist unless their components exist together with one another, as is evident from a bed and similar things; and the parts of an argument do not exist together with one another. When we state the first premise, neither the other premise nor the conclusion exists yet. When we state the second, the first premise no longer exists and the conclusion does not exist yet. When we pronounce the conclusion, its premises no longer exist. So the parts of an argument do not exist together with one another, and hence the argument itself will not seem to exist either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-683ddfa2942c4df1822017cf8569d236","ordinal":386,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.145","text":"\n\nApart from this, the conclusive argument is inapprehensible. It is judged by the following in the conditional, and the following in the conditional is the subject of undecidable disagreement and is perhaps inapprehensible, as we pointed out in the discussion of the sign. So the conclusive argument will be inapprehensible too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a64736f6d2f84a659d55a04848efe727","ordinal":387,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.146","text":"\n\nNow the dialecticians say that an argument comes out inconclusive through disconnection, or through deficiency, or through being propounded in an invalid form, or through redundancy. It is inconclusive through disconnection, for example, when the premises have no connection with one another and with the conclusion, as in this one: “If it is day, it is light; but wheat is being sold in the market;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c00e5a5f2e03439eb8a2a03073bffe69","ordinal":388,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.147","text":"\n\ntherefore Dion is walking.” It is inconclusive through redundancy when a premise is found to be redundant for the argument’s deduction. For example: “If it is day, it is light; but it is day, and Dion is walking too; therefore it is light.” It is inconclusive through being propounded in an invalid form when the form of the argument is not conclusive. For example, while these arguments, they say, are syllogistic—“If it is day, it is light; but it is day; therefore it is light,” and “If it is day, ⟨it is light⟩; but it is not light; therefore it is not day”—this one is inconclusive: “If it is day, it is light; but it is light; therefore it is day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dadd2b30e93142d993a03e1c25b55df5","ordinal":389,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.148","text":"\n\nFor the conditional announces that, if its antecedent holds, the consequent holds too. So it is reasonable that when the antecedent is added as a premise, the consequent is inferred as well, and when the consequent is denied, the antecedent is denied as well; for if the antecedent held, the consequent would hold too. But when the consequent is added, the antecedent is not necessarily posited. The conditional did not promise that the antecedent follows the consequent, only that the consequent follows the antecedent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e0ed5f9382454506a6479efa4c81f378","ordinal":390,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.149","text":"\n\nThat is why an argument that deduces the consequent from a conditional and its antecedent is said to be syllogistic, as is one that deduces the opposite of the antecedent from a conditional and the opposite of the consequent. But an argument that deduces the antecedent from a conditional and its consequent is inconclusive, like the one just mentioned. That is why, even when its premises are true, it deduces something false, if it is stated at night by lamplight. The conditional “if it is day, it is light” is true, and so is the additional premise “but it is light,” but the conclusion “therefore it is day” is false."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-54f1b8b48f4846c3ab0d4c67b60aca42","ordinal":391,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.150","text":"\n\nAn argument is faulty through omission when something needed for deducing the conclusion is left out. For example, this argument, as they think, is sound: “Wealth is either good or bad or indifferent; but it is neither bad nor indifferent; therefore it is good.” But this one is faulty through deficiency: “Wealth is either good or bad; but it is not bad; therefore it is good.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-533321186a8d42a89f0fa0875356503e","ordinal":392,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.151","text":"\n\nIf, then, I show that, according to them, no kind of inconclusive argument can be distinguished from conclusive ones, I will have shown that the conclusive argument is inapprehensible. Their endless chatter about dialectic, current ⟨among⟩ them, will then be superfluous. I show it as follows."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-21feab0086d34dcb8945aa686c6339f8","ordinal":393,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.152","text":"\n\nThe argument inconclusive through disconnection was said to be recognized by the fact that its premises have no connection with one another ⟨and⟩ with the conclusion. Judgment of the conditional must come before knowledge of this connection, and the conditional cannot be decided, as I have argued. So the argument inconclusive through disconnection will also be ⟨indistinguishable⟩."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f272059913dc443ca803ae6a69331f9c","ordinal":394,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.153","text":"\n\nTake someone who says that some argument is inconclusive through disconnection. If he offers mere assertion, he will find set against him the assertion opposed to his. If he proves it by an argument, he will be told that this argument must first be conclusive, and only then prove that the premises of the argument said to be disconnected are unconnected. But we will not know whether it is probative, since we have no agreed way of judging the conditional by which to judge whether the conclusion follows from the conjunction of the argument’s premises. So on this count too we will be unable to distinguish the argument said to be invalid through disconnection from conclusive ones."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8aa7a16ed84c436482edaeb92fd412cd","ordinal":395,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.154","text":"\n\nWe will say the same to someone who says that an argument is invalid through being propounded in a bad form. Whoever argues that some form is invalid will not have an agreed conclusive argument through which he can deduce what he says."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-709b2c5ae2b6430b97cc572f79fd36c3","ordinal":396,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.155","text":"\n\nWith this we have also, in effect, answered those who try to show that arguments are inconclusive through deficiency. For if the complete ⟨and⟩ finished argument is non-evident, the deficient one will be non-evident too. Further, someone who wants to show by argument that some argument is deficient has no agreed way of judging the conditional, by which he could judge the following in the argument he states. So he will not be able to say, with sound judgment and correctly, that it is deficient."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-35ab648d43e047999d6b91ce41c767e5","ordinal":397,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.156","text":"\n\nMoreover, the argument said to be invalid through redundancy cannot be distinguished from probative ones. As far as redundancy goes, even the indemonstrables the Stoics talk so much about will turn out to be inconclusive. And if they are done away with, the whole of dialectic is overturned. For these are the arguments that, they say, need no proof to establish themselves, but are probative ⟨by⟩ making the other arguments conclusive. That they are redundant will be clear once we have set out the indemonstrables and then inferred what we are claiming."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a0dd264c2da74a309d3d6bc5e0a1ba6c","ordinal":398,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.157","text":"\n\n⟨Now⟩ they dream up many indemonstrables, but they chiefly set out these five, to which all the rest seem to be reduced. First, ⟨the one⟩ that deduces the consequent from a conditional and its antecedent, for example “If it is day, it is light; but it is day; therefore it is light.” Second, the one that deduces the opposite of the antecedent from a conditional and the opposite of the consequent, for example “If it is day, it is light; but it is not light;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7fb8c18480e045ddaeb0001a7e6c8e91","ordinal":399,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.158","text":"\n\ntherefore it is not day.” Third, the one that deduces, from a negated conjunction and one of its conjuncts, the opposite of the other conjunct, for example “Not both: it is day and it is night; but it is day; therefore it is not night.” Fourth, the one that deduces, from a disjunction and one of its disjuncts, the opposite of the other, for example “Either it is day or it is night; but it is day; therefore it is not night.” Fifth, the one that deduces, from a disjunction and the opposite of one of its disjuncts, the other disjunct, for example “Either it is day or it is night; but it is not night; therefore it is day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7ced010a0aac4bd2aa797195059ad22b","ordinal":400,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.159","text":"\n\nThese, then, are the much-talked-about indemonstrables, and all of them seem to me to be inconclusive through redundancy. To start right away with the first: either it is agreed that “it is light” follows “it is day,” its antecedent, in the conditional “if it is day, it is light,” or this is non-evident. If it is non-evident, we will not grant the conditional as agreed. But if it is evident that if “it is day” holds, “it is light” will necessarily hold too, then once we have said that it is day, it is also deduced that it is light. So an argument like “It is day; therefore it is light” suffices, and the conditional “if it is day, it is light” is redundant."}]}